From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
Date: Tue, 6 Dec 1994 23:27:44 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-249] ECO: The economics of extinction

An article in the Nov 3 Nature analyzes when it is economically
better to exterminate a species than to continue harvesting it at
sustainable rates.

Under previous, deterministic harvesting models, it is more profitable
to exterminate a species if its growth rate is lower than the discount
rate (which is, I gather, the rate of return on invested money).
That's because the gain you get from future harvests of the species is
less than the gain you would get if you killed them all now and put the
profits in the bank.

The Nature article shows that species extermination is economically
optimal even when growth rate is a little higher than the discount
rate, because population fluctuations make extinction likely and so
reduce the expected future harvest.

A commentary on the article suggests that whales, tropical hardwoods,
and elephants fall into the category of things with growth rates slow
enough that it is more profitable to kill them all now.

These analyses assume that one person has a monopoly on the species.
If, as in reality, many people can harvest them, the point at which
extermination is optimal for the harvester comes much sooner.

Phil goetz@cs.buffalo.edu, eco-Nazi, strikes again

