From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Wed, 7 Dec 94 4:35:12 GMT
Subject: [#94-12-252] Singularity: Bah! Humbug!

Here's what life will *really* be like after the Singularity.

Nothing will fit anything else--it will be like computer incompatibility,
except that it won't just be computers. Your mind, your body, your
language, your society, your desk-top manufactury and the various
bit of your computer will *all* be incompatible.

While there will be fortunes to be made in getting things to work 
together, it will always be easier to invent new systems that have
a lot of promise but don't fit that to get old systems to be
compatible with each other.

Compatibility problems may well be what turns those exponential
curves into s-curves. I don't know that the Singularity will be
brought to a screeching halt, but there might be plateaus, perhaps
even plateaus of increasing length.

If they can sustain their niches, there might be "Amish" who
stop at various tech levels just so that they have a chance
to do something other than trying to get things to fit together.

You will live 10,000 years, and spend at least two thirds of 
that time trying to get things to work.

Corolary: Dave Barry will be the richest entity in the solar
system.

Nancy Lebovitz



