From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 00:25:36 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-255] EVOL: Is enhancement like peeling an onion? (Re: upgrading)

>Considering that the human brain is a hack, (With 3 distinct
>layers, reptilian, mammalian, and the most recently evolved,
>consciousness providing layer), and considering that the lower two
>regions are an abundant source of irrational behavior (that are
>tolerated, even expressed in the upper); has anyone pondered the
>stability of this structure were it to be upgraded?
>
>Mark Fischer

I ask:  Is the mind an ear of corn or an onion?
Start peeling away sources of irrational behavior:  testosterone,
PMS, pain, discomfort, tiredness, anger, sorrow,...
Will you eventually reach some golden Platonic mind at the center,
or will there be nothing left when you peel away the last layer?

I think that joys and sorrows make existence worthwhile.
But emotions most likely developed as evolutionary adaptations
which enhanced survival and reproduction.  My theory is that emotions
encourage actions to solve animal problems.

But when we remake the mind, we will not have the same priorities.
I want to transmit memes, not genes.
Sex, for instance, will be obsolete:  there will be better, more
controlled ways to reproduce and to experience pleasure.  All the
emotions connected with sex will become obsolete, and a hindrance
to rational thought.  Similarly, the good feelings we get from
eating food sometimes cause us to eat too much, or the wrong foods;
we will suppress them at times.  We may attach them to something else
entirely, such as Georgian architecture, but we will want, in the name
of rationality, to be able to turn them off.

When we cut away all the evolved responses to situations that we no
longer worry about, what will be left of our emotions?

The question is really, What do we want to be?  If all we want is
rationality, that's all we'll get.  But there is no logical reason
to take pleasure in existence; pleasure is a fortunate accident
of evolution.

A related question is whether we will still have the same emotional
depth if we live in a world in which we have little conflict over
resources.  In Cognitive Science 16(4) (Oct-Dec 92), Anna Wierzbicka
analyzes the enabling conditions of different emotions.  In general,
she claims that positive emotions often result from overcoming negative
situations.  As we eliminate more and more negative situations, we may
eliminate our opportunities to _feel_.  Imagine someone living in a VR
world, with unlimited "physical" resources (the only real limit is
computation time, and you can always get more by running slower).  She
experiences no frustration, no competition.  What will make her feel happy?

(Someone else has done work similar to Anna's, maybe John Pollock,
but I forget who now.)

A theorem-proving computer program, even with superhuman intelligence,
would still have no id, and no will to exist.


Phil goetz@cs.buffalo.edu

